# Code Style

- `const` over `let` — always
- Early returns over deeply nested `if/else`
- No magic strings or numbers — use enums or constants
- No `any` type — use `unknown` and narrow, or define proper interfaces
- Async/await everywhere — no raw Promises or callbacks
- One class per file, file name matches class name in kebab-case
- Imports ordered: NestJS > third-party > local
- No barrel files (`index.ts`) except in `common/`
- No commented-out code in production files

## Security

- Never expose internal DB IDs, password hashes, tokens in API responses
- Hash passwords with bcrypt (cost factor 12)
- `@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)` on protected routes
- Role-based access via `@Roles()` decorator + `RolesGuard`
- Rate limiting on auth endpoints (`@nestjs/throttler`)
- Helmet middleware enabled
- CORS configured explicitly — never `origin: '*'` in production

## Logging

- Use NestJS `Logger` — never `console.log`
- Each service: `private readonly logger = new Logger(ServiceName.name)`
- Log on create/update/delete operations
- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII)

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

- Returning raw Prisma models from controllers (leaks sensitive fields)
- Business logic in controllers
- `findMany()` without `take` limit
- Silent error catching: `catch(e) {}`
- Using `@Res()` (breaks interceptors and exception filters)
